{"highest":2,"ids":{"security lens, medium: ANSI escape sequences from an attacker-controlled GitHub comment survive partial stripping and are emitted to the user's terminal via printf. (core/claim/claim.sh: \"holder_value=\"$(sed -e 's/^\"holder\":[[:space:]]*\"\\(.*\\)\"$/\\1/' \\             -e 's/\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*[mK]//g' <<< \"$raw_holder\" || true)\"\"; happens when: An attacker who can post or edit a comment on the GitHub issue embeds ANSI escape bytes (cursor movement, screen-clear, OSC title sequences, etc.) in the <!-- zbuild-holder: --> field. The sed pattern only strips CSI sequences ending in m or K; other ESC-prefixed sequences survive into _ZBUILD_CLAIM_HOLDER and are then sent to stderr via printf '%s\\n' \"${_ZBUILD_CLAIM_HOLDER:-unknown}\" >&2, where any terminal emulator will interpret them.; fix: code; ACCEPTED IF: The holder string extracted from outside input is stripped of all ANSI/terminal escape sequences before being printed to stderr, using a strip that is at least as broad as the event bus's _eb_strip_ansi (which covers CSI sequences ending in any letter or ~ and bare ESC-X pairs).)":"RLSECURITY-1","security lens, low: The holder is JSON-encoded by escaping only double-quote characters, leaving literal backslashes unescaped and producing structurally invalid JSON. (plugins/claim-coordinator/github-labels/plugin.sh: \"escaped_holder=\"$(printf '%s' \"$holder\" | sed 's/\"/\\\\\"/g' || true)\"\"; happens when: A holder string that contains a backslash (e.g. a Windows path or any attacker-modified holder) generates output such as {\"acquired\": false, \"holder\": \"foo\\bar\"}, which is invalid JSON (bare backslash). Downstream extraction in claim.sh uses grep/sed rather than jq, so there is no current injection path, but any future JSON consumer of the coordinator's stdout would mis-parse the response.; fix: code; ACCEPTED IF: The holder embedded in the JSON stdout of claim_coordinator_claim is fully JSON-string-safe: at minimum backslash is escaped to \\\\ before double-quote is escaped, so the value could be re-parsed by a standards-conforming JSON tool.)":"RLSECURITY-2"}}
